<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Workshops</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ateliers nationaux</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National Workshops were a public works scheme set up by the Provisional Government of the French Second Republic in February 1848 to give work to the unemployed of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Over 100,000 men enrolled, alarming conservatives. Their closure by the elected assembly in June 1848 provoked the June Days uprising, which was crushed by General Cavaignac with thousands killed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-workshops.jpg" alt="National Workshops" width="320" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ateliers_nationaux_au_Champ-de-Mars_(Bouton).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
